OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Nonsingular-systems-of-equations-over-arbitrary-groups-October-5-2026", "title": "Nonsingular systems of equations over arbitrary groups", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Nonsingular-systems-of-equations-over-arbitrary-groups-October-5-2026/nonsingular-systems-over-arbitrary-groups.pdf", "family": "256", "family_title": "Nonsingular systems of equations over arbitrary groups", "abstract_html": "Every finite nonsingular system of equations over an arbitrary group has a simultaneous solution in an overgroup. This proves Howie's conjecture on nonsingular systems.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If \\(\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb Q}A=m\\), then the canonical homomorphism \\( G\\longrightarrow (G*F_n)/\\langle\\!\\langle w_1,\\ldots,w_m\\rangle\\!\\rangle \\) is injective.", "statement": "<p>**Theorem 1 (Nonsingular systems; original Theorem 1).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\) be any group, let \\(1\\le m\\le n\\), and let \\(F_n=F(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(w_1,\\ldots,w_m\\in G*F_n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define the exponent-sum matrix \\(A=(a_{ij})\\in M_{m\\times n}(\\mathbb Z)\\) by the homomorphism\n  \\[\n  G*F_n\\longrightarrow\\mathbb Z^n,\\qquad\n  G\\longmapsto0,\\quad x_j\\longmapsto e_j,\\quad\n  w_i\\longmapsto(a_{i1},\\ldots,a_{in}).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The system \\(w_i=1\\), \\(1\\le i\\le m\\), is called nonsingular when \\(\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb Q}A=m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\langle\\!\\langle w_1,\\ldots,w_m\\rangle\\!\\rangle\\) for their normal closure in \\(G*F_n\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb Q}A=m\\), then the canonical homomorphism\n\\[\nG\\longrightarrow (G*F_n)/\\langle\\!\\langle w_1,\\ldots,w_m\\rangle\\!\\rangle\n\\]\nis injective. Equivalently, there is a group containing an isomorphic copy of \\(G\\) in which all the equations \\(w_i=1\\) have a simultaneous solution.</p>", "proof": "<p>Cyclically conjugating the relators changes neither their normal closure nor their exponent sums. Each row of \\(A\\) is nonzero, so every relator has a variable occurrence and can be written in the form (4.1), with \\(N_i\\ge1\\).</p><p>Let \\(g\\in G\\) lie in the normal closure of the relators. By definition, there is a finite expression\n\\[\ng=\\prod_{\\alpha=1}^u\nb_\\alpha w_{i_\\alpha}^{\\epsilon_\\alpha}b_\\alpha^{-1}\n\\]\nin \\(G*F_n\\). If \\(u=0\\), then \\(g=1\\). Assume \\(u&gt;0\\). Using cyclic representatives only changes the corresponding conjugators.</p><p>Apply Proposition 5. It gives signed word disks and disjoint bands embedded in the interior of \\(\\Delta\\), together with the coefficient map \\(v\\) on the punctured disk. Every connected component of their union is planar.</p><p>It remains to account for several components nested inside one another. Each connected component has one exterior boundary circle whose Jordan disk contains the component; its other boundary circles enclose the inner complementary disks. The exterior Jordan disks of distinct components are disjoint or nested.</p><p>Choose a component whose exterior disk is minimal under inclusion. Its inner complementary disks contain no other component, and hence contain no missing word disk. The coefficient map \\(v\\) is already defined on each of them. Their boundary words are therefore trivial in \\(G\\).</p><p>Proposition 4 implies that the exterior boundary word of the chosen component is also trivial. Its exact boundary loop extends to a map of its Jordan disk into \\(Y\\). Replace \\(v\\) inside that disk by this extension. The two maps agree on the boundary, so they paste continuously. No other component lies in the disk, and the coefficient labels and band constants on all remaining components are unchanged.</p><p>Repeating this finite procedure removes all components. The result is a map\n\\[\n\\Delta\\longrightarrow Y\n\\]\nextending the original outer loop for \\(g\\). Consequently \\(g=1\\) in \\(G\\). Thus\n\\[\nG\\cap\\langle\\!\\langle w_1,\\ldots,w_m\\rangle\\!\\rangle=\\{1\\},\n\\]\nwhich proves the asserted injectivity.</p><p>The equivalence with solvability follows from the universal property of the quotient. If the canonical map is injective, the quotient itself supplies the overgroup and the images of the free generators supply the solution. Conversely, any solution induces a homomorphism from the quotient whose restriction to \\(G\\) is injective.</p><p>**Arbitrary-set consequence (original Remark 5.1).**\nThe finite statement also implies the version with arbitrary sets of variables and equations whose finite-support exponent rows are linearly independent over \\(\\mathbb Q\\). Any element of \\(G\\) killed in the quotient has a normal-closure expression involving only finitely many relators and finitely many variables, including those in its conjugators. The corresponding finite exponent matrix still has independent rows, so the finite statement just proved makes that element trivial.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is \\(X\\in D\\) such that \\( \\sum_{j=1}^n\\dim_{\\mathbb C}\\ker(F_j(X)-I_{d_j}) \\ge \\sum_{i=1}^rk_i. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Theorem 2 (Simultaneous fixed spaces; original Theorem 2).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(r\\ge0\\), \\(n\\ge1\\), and let \\(k_1,\\ldots,k_r\\) be positive integers.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(1\\le j\\le n\\), let \\(\\mathcal B_j\\) be a finite ordered list of pairs \\((i,s)\\), where \\(1\\le i\\le r\\) and \\(s\\in\\{1,-1\\}\\); repetitions are allowed.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  d_j=\\sum_{(i,s)\\in\\mathcal B_j}k_i,\\qquad\n  b_{ij}=\\sum_{\\substack{(i',s)\\in\\mathcal B_j\\\\i'=i}}s,\\qquad\n  B=(b_{ij}).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb Q}B=r\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For arbitrary \\(P_j\\in U(d_j)\\), define\n  \\[\n  D=\\prod_{i=1}^rU(k_i),\\qquad\n  F_j(X)=\\operatorname{diag}_{(i,s)\\in\\mathcal B_j}(X_i^s)P_j,\n  \\qquad X=(X_1,\\ldots,X_r)\\in D.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Here \\(U(0)\\) and an empty product of groups are points, and the fixed-space dimension in dimension zero is zero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(X\\in D\\) such that\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^n\\dim_{\\mathbb C}\\ker(F_j(X)-I_{d_j})\n\\ge \\sum_{i=1}^rk_i.\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The case \\(r=0\\) is immediate. Assume \\(r&gt;0\\), and reorder the rows so that\n\\[\nk_1\\ge\\cdots\\ge k_r.\n\\]</p><p>Proposition 2 supplies distinct columns \\(j_1,\\ldots,j_r\\), target dimensions \\(l_j\\), and a class\n\\[\n\\Omega=\\prod_{j=1}^nF_j^*\\alpha(d_j,l_j)\n\\in H^{\\sum_i k_i^2}(D;\\mathbb Q)\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\n\\sum_jl_j=\\sum_ik_i,\\qquad\n\\sum_jl_j^2=\\dim_{\\mathbb R}D,\\qquad\n\\langle\\Omega,[D]\\rangle\\ne0.\n\\]</p><p>Let\n\\[\nF=(F_1,\\ldots,F_n):D\\longrightarrow\\prod_jU(d_j),\n\\]\nand let\n\\[\np_{\\mathrm{tot}}=\\prod_jp_{d_j,l_j}:\n\\prod_jR(d_j,l_j)\\longrightarrow\\prod_jU(d_j).\n\\]\nUp to the harmless orientation sign, \\(\\Omega\\) is the pullback by \\(F\\) of the Poincaré dual of the cycle defined by \\(p_{\\mathrm{tot}}\\).</p><p>By Proposition 1, its nonzero evaluation implies that these two maps have intersecting images. At an intersection, \\(F_j(X)\\) fixes an \\(l_j\\)-plane for every \\(j\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^n\\dim_{\\mathbb C}\\ker(F_j(X)-I_{d_j})\n\\ge\\sum_jl_j=\\sum_{i=1}^rk_i,\n\\]\nwhich is (2.1).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are compatible primitive generators \\( H^*(U(d);\\mathbb Q)=\\Lambda(e_{1,d},\\ldots,e_{d,d}), \\qquad |e_{a,d}|=2a-1, \\) such that a coordinate-block inclusion \\(\\iota_{k,d}:U(k)\\to U(d)\\) satisfies …", "statement": "<p>**Unitary cohomology and fixed-subspace incidence classes.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">All cohomology in this proposition has coefficients in \\(\\mathbb Q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(d\\ge0\\) and \\(0\\le l\\le d\\), define\n  \\[\n  R(d,l)=\n  \\{(W,E):W\\in U(d),\\ E\\in\\operatorname{Gr}_l(\\mathbb C^d),\\\n  W|_E=I_E\\},\n  \\qquad p_{d,l}(W,E)=W.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Once orientations are chosen, set\n  \\[\n  \\alpha(d,l)=\\operatorname{PD}\\bigl((p_{d,l})_*[R(d,l)]\\bigr).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are compatible primitive generators\n\\[\nH^*(U(d);\\mathbb Q)=\\Lambda(e_{1,d},\\ldots,e_{d,d}),\n\\qquad |e_{a,d}|=2a-1,\n\\]\nsuch that a coordinate-block inclusion \\(\\iota_{k,d}:U(k)\\to U(d)\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\iota_{k,d}^*e_{a,d}=\n\\begin{cases}\ne_{a,k},&amp;a\\le k,\\\\\n0,&amp;a&gt;k,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\ngroup multiplication \\(\\mu\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\mu^*e_{a,d}=e_{a,d}\\otimes1+1\\otimes e_{a,d},\n\\]\nand inversion sends \\(e_{a,d}\\) to \\(-e_{a,d}\\).</p><p>The manifold \\(R(d,l)\\) is compact, oriented, and without boundary, of real dimension \\(d^2-l^2\\). Its image under \\(p_{d,l}\\) is exactly\n\\[\n\\{W\\in U(d):\\dim_{\\mathbb C}\\ker(W-I_d)\\ge l\\}.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\alpha(d,l)\n=c_{d,l}e_{1,d}\\cdots e_{l,d}\n+\\text{terms with fewer than \\(l\\) generators},\n\\qquad c_{d,l}\\ne0,\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\nwith \\(\\alpha(d,0)=1\\) and \\(c_{d,0}=1\\).</p><p>For finitely many pairs \\((d_j,l_j)\\), a closed oriented manifold \\(M\\) of dimension \\(\\sum_jl_j^2\\), and maps \\(f_j:M\\to U(d_j)\\), the condition\n\\[\n\\left\\langle\\prod_jf_j^*\\alpha(d_j,l_j),[M]\\right\\rangle\\ne0\n\\]\nimplies the existence of \\(x\\in M\\) such that\n\\[\n\\dim_{\\mathbb C}\\ker(f_j(x)-I_{d_j})\\ge l_j\n\\quad\\text{for every }j.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 1.1 gives the compatible primitive exterior generators. Lemma 1.2 constructs the incidence classes and gives their intersection interpretation. Lemma 1.3 gives their nonzero maximal-length terms.</p><p>For maps \\(f_j:M\\to U(d_j)\\), apply the product intersection interpretation of Lemma 1.2 to\n\\[\n(f_1,\\ldots,f_n)\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\prod_jp_{d_j,l_j}.\n\\]\nA nonzero evaluation of \\(\\prod_jf_j^*\\alpha(d_j,l_j)\\) implies that these maps have intersecting images. At an intersection, every \\(f_j(x)\\) fixes an \\(l_j\\)-plane.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Generators \\(e_{a,d}\\), \\(1\\le a\\le d\\), can be chosen so that \\( H^*(U(d);\\mathbb Q)=\\Lambda(e_{1,d},\\ldots,e_{d,d}), \\qquad |e_{a,d}|=2a-1, \\) …", "statement": "<p>**Compatible primitive generators.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use rational cohomology.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\iota_{k,d}:U(k)\\to U(d)\\) denote inclusion as a coordinate block, with the identity on its complement.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mu:U(d)\\times U(d)\\to U(d)\\) be multiplication.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Generators \\(e_{a,d}\\), \\(1\\le a\\le d\\), can be chosen so that\n\\[\nH^*(U(d);\\mathbb Q)=\\Lambda(e_{1,d},\\ldots,e_{d,d}),\n\\qquad |e_{a,d}|=2a-1,\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\iota_{k,d}^*e_{a,d}=\n\\begin{cases}\ne_{a,k},&amp;a\\le k,\\\\\n0,&amp;a&gt;k,\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mu^*e_{a,d}=e_{a,d}\\otimes1+1\\otimes e_{a,d}.\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\nInversion pulls \\(e_{a,d}\\) back to \\(-e_{a,d}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>These properties follow together from the last-column bundle\n\\[\nU(d-1)\\longrightarrow U(d)\\longrightarrow S^{2d-1}.\n\\]</p><p>Start with the degree-one generator of \\(U(1)=S^1\\). For \\(d\\ge2\\), the base is simply connected, so the coefficient system in the multiplicative Serre spectral sequence is constant. Its second page is\n\\[\nE_2^{p,q}\n=H^p(S^{2d-1};\\mathbb Q)\\otimes H^q(U(d-1);\\mathbb Q).\n\\]\nIts only nonzero columns are \\(p=0,2d-1\\). The only possible differential between them is \\(d_{2d-1}\\), which sends fiber degree \\(q\\) to degree \\(q-2d+2\\). Each fiber generator has degree at most \\(2d-3\\), so its differential has negative target degree and vanishes. The differential vanishes on all their products by the product rule. Consequently the spectral sequence collapses.</p><p>Its edge maps show that restriction to \\(U(d-1)\\) is an isomorphism in degrees below \\(2d-1\\), and that the pullback of the sphere’s top class is nonzero. Lift each earlier generator in its degree, and take this sphere pullback as \\(e_{d,d}\\). Their exterior products give the basis in (2.4): they give that basis in the associated graded algebra supplied by the spectral sequence, and their squares vanish because they have odd degree and the coefficients are rational. This also proves compatibility with the standard inclusions.</p><p>To check primitivity, subtract the two summands on the right of (2.6) from its left side. The difference restricts to zero on either factor at the identity, so every term in its Künneth decomposition has positive degree in both factors. Each such degree is at most \\(2d-2\\). Restriction to \\(U(d-1)\\times U(d-1)\\) is injective on all these bidegrees, by the edge-map isomorphism just proved.</p><p>On this product the difference vanishes: for \\(a&lt;d\\) this is the induction hypothesis, and for \\(a=d\\) it follows because \\(e_{d,d}\\) is pulled back by the last-column projection, which is constant on the fiber. This proves (2.6); the case \\(d=1\\) is immediate.</p><p>Coordinate-block inclusions in other positions are conjugate to the standard one, and conjugation is homotopic to the identity in the connected group \\(U(d)\\). Finally, inversion sends \\(e_{a,d}\\) to \\(-e_{a,d}\\), by pulling (2.6) back along \\(X\\mapsto(X,X^{-1})\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The space \\(R(d,l)\\) is a compact oriented smooth manifold without boundary, with \\( \\dim_{\\mathbb R}R(d,l)=d^2-l^2, \\qquad \\alpha(d,l)\\in H^{l^2}(U(d);\\mathbb Q). \\) Its image under \\(p_{d,l}\\) consists exactly of the …", "statement": "<p>**The incidence manifold and its intersection interpretation.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(0\\le l\\le d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(R(d,l)\\), \\(p_{d,l}\\), and \\(\\alpha(d,l)\\) as in Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The space \\(R(d,l)\\) is a compact oriented smooth manifold without boundary, with\n\\[\n\\dim_{\\mathbb R}R(d,l)=d^2-l^2,\n\\qquad\n\\alpha(d,l)\\in H^{l^2}(U(d);\\mathbb Q).\n\\]\nIts image under \\(p_{d,l}\\) consists exactly of the matrices having an \\(l\\)-dimensional fixed subspace.</p><p>If \\(M\\) is a closed oriented manifold of dimension \\(l^2\\) and \\(f:M\\to U(d)\\), then\n\\[\n\\langle f^*\\alpha(d,l),[M]\\rangle\n\\]\nis the intersection number of \\(f\\) and \\(p_{d,l}\\). Disjoint images give intersection number zero. The corresponding assertions hold for products of these maps and classes.</p>", "proof": "<p>Over a subspace \\(E\\in\\operatorname{Gr}_l(\\mathbb C^d)\\), the possible \\(W\\) are precisely the unitary transformations of \\(E^\\perp\\), extended by the identity on \\(E\\). Orthonormal frame charts therefore make \\(R(d,l)\\) a smooth bundle over the Grassmannian with fiber \\(U(d-l)\\). It is compact and without boundary, and\n\\[\n\\dim_{\\mathbb R}R(d,l)\n=2l(d-l)+(d-l)^2=d^2-l^2.\n\\]</p><p>The complex orientation of the Grassmannian and an orientation of \\(U(d-l)\\) orient this bundle: changes of frame act on the fiber by conjugation and preserve orientation. Poincaré duality applies to the homology class pushed forward by \\(p_{d,l}\\); the image itself need not be a submanifold. That image is exactly the set where\n\\[\n\\dim_{\\mathbb C}\\ker(W-I_d)\\ge l.\n\\]</p><p>When \\(f\\) and \\(p_{d,l}\\) are transverse, the intersection number is the signed count of pairs\n\\[\n(x,(W,E))\\quad\\text{with}\\quad f(x)=p_{d,l}(W,E).\n\\]\nThis follows by intersecting \\(f\\times p_{d,l}\\) with the diagonal of \\(U(d)\\times U(d)\\): transversality makes its inverse image a compact oriented zero-manifold, whose signed count gives the intersection pairing.</p><p>In particular, disjoint images give intersection number zero: disjoint maps from compact manifolds have disjoint sufficiently small transverse perturbations. The same interpretation holds for products of these maps and classes.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is \\(c_{d,l}\\in\\mathbb Q\\setminus\\{0\\}\\) such that \\( \\alpha(d,l) =c_{d,l}e_{1,d}\\cdots e_{l,d} +\\text{terms with fewer than \\(l\\) generators}.", "statement": "<p>**The maximal-length term of the incidence class.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(0\\le l\\le d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the generators from Lemma 1.1 and the incidence class from Lemma 1.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(c_{d,l}\\in\\mathbb Q\\setminus\\{0\\}\\) such that\n\\[\n\\alpha(d,l)\n=c_{d,l}e_{1,d}\\cdots e_{l,d}\n+\\text{terms with fewer than \\(l\\) generators}.\n\\]\nFor \\(l=0\\), \\(\\alpha(d,0)=1\\) and \\(c_{d,0}=1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(l=0\\), the map \\(p_{d,0}\\) is the identity.</p><p>For \\(l&gt;0\\), a product of \\(u\\) distinct generators has degree at least\n\\[\n1+3+\\cdots+(2u-1)=u^2.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\alpha(d,l)\\) has degree \\(l^2\\), its expansion contains at most \\(l\\) generators in each term, and the sole possible term of length \\(l\\) is \\(e_{1,d}\\cdots e_{l,d}\\).</p><p>To show that its coefficient is nonzero, consider\n\\[\nf:U(l)\\longrightarrow U(d),\\qquad\nf(X)=X\\oplus(-I_{d-l}).\n\\]\nThere is exactly one intersection pair for \\(f\\) and \\(p_{d,l}\\):\n\\[\nX=I_l,\\qquad\nW=W_0=I_l\\oplus(-I_{d-l}),\\qquad\nE=E_0=\\mathbb C^l\\oplus0.\n\\]\nIndeed, the second summand has no fixed vectors, while an \\(l\\)-dimensional fixed space in the first summand forces \\(X=I_l\\).</p><p>This intersection is transverse. Identify the tangent space at \\(W_0\\) with skew-Hermitian matrices by right multiplication by \\(W_0^{-1}\\). Variations of \\(f\\) fill the upper-left block; variations of \\(p_{d,l}\\) in the fiber over \\(E_0\\) fill the lower-right block. For a linear map \\(Z:E_0\\to E_0^\\perp\\), put\n\\[\nT=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;-Z^*\\\\ Z&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThe path\n\\[\n(e^{tT}W_0e^{-tT},e^{tT}E_0)\n\\]\nlies in \\(R(d,l)\\), and its image under \\(p_{d,l}\\) has tangent vector\n\\[\n[T,W_0]W_0^{-1}=2T.\n\\]\nThese vectors supply every off-diagonal direction. The unique intersection therefore has multiplicity \\(1\\) or \\(-1\\).</p><p>The map \\(f\\) is homotopic to the coordinate inclusion, so (2.5) computes its pullback. Terms with an index above \\(l\\) vanish; any remaining term with fewer than \\(l\\) generators has degree less than\n\\[\n\\dim U(l)=l^2.\n\\]\nOnly \\(e_{1,d}\\cdots e_{l,d}\\) can account for the nonzero intersection number. Thus \\(c_{d,l}\\ne0\\). The same argument includes \\(l=d\\), when the complementary blocks are absent.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are distinct columns \\(j_1,\\ldots,j_r\\) such that \\( \\det B[1,\\ldots,t\\mid j_1,\\ldots,j_t]\\ne0 \\qquad(1\\le t\\le r). \\) Define \\( l_{j_t}=k_t\\quad(1\\le t\\le r),\\qquad l_j=0\\quad\\text{for unchosen columns}. \\) Then …", "statement": "<p>**Target dimensions and the nonzero intersection class.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(r&gt;0\\), \\(n\\ge1\\), and let\n  \\[\n  k_1\\ge\\cdots\\ge k_r&gt;0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal B_j\\), \\(d_j\\), \\(b_{ij}\\), \\(B\\), \\(P_j\\), \\(D\\), and \\(F_j\\) be as in Theorem 2, with \\(\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb Q}B=r\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the incidence classes \\(\\alpha(d,l)\\) of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(B[1,\\ldots,t\\mid j_1,\\ldots,j_t]\\) for the indicated square submatrix.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are distinct columns \\(j_1,\\ldots,j_r\\) such that\n\\[\n\\det B[1,\\ldots,t\\mid j_1,\\ldots,j_t]\\ne0\n\\qquad(1\\le t\\le r).\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nl_{j_t}=k_t\\quad(1\\le t\\le r),\\qquad\nl_j=0\\quad\\text{for unchosen columns}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nl_j\\le d_j,\\qquad\n\\sum_jl_j=\\sum_ik_i,\\qquad\n\\sum_jl_j^2=\\sum_ik_i^2=\\dim_{\\mathbb R}D,\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\nand the top-degree class\n\\[\n\\Omega=\\prod_{j=1}^nF_j^*\\alpha(d_j,l_j)\n\\]\nhas nonzero evaluation on \\([D]\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.1 supplies the columns and target dimensions. Lemma 2.2 computes the resulting top-degree class and proves that its evaluation on \\([D]\\) is nonzero.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Columns \\(j_1,\\ldots,j_r\\) satisfying (2.2) exist.", "statement": "<p>**Allocation by nested nonzero minors.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the matrix \\(B\\), ordered sizes \\(k_1\\ge\\cdots\\ge k_r&gt;0\\), and block dimensions \\(d_j\\) of Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Columns \\(j_1,\\ldots,j_r\\) satisfying (2.2) exist. For any such choice, the dimensions \\(l_j\\) defined in Proposition 2 satisfy all the inequalities and equalities in (2.3).</p>", "proof": "<p>The first \\(t\\) rows of \\(B\\) have rank \\(t\\). The columns chosen at the previous step remain independent on these rows, since their restrictions to the first \\(t-1\\) rows are independent; they can therefore be extended by one column. This constructs the required nested nonzero minors.</p><p>For \\(j=j_t\\), the nonzero minor in (2.2) implies \\(b_{i,j_t}\\ne0\\) for some \\(i\\le t\\). The list \\(\\mathcal B_{j_t}\\) thus contains a block of size \\(k_i\\ge k_t\\), so \\(l_{j_t}=k_t\\le d_{j_t}\\). Unchosen columns have \\(l_j=0\\). Finally,\n\\[\n\\sum_jl_j=\\sum_ik_i,\\qquad\n\\sum_jl_j^2=\\sum_ik_i^2=\\dim_{\\mathbb R}D.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(1\\le a\\le d_j\\), \\( F_j^*e_{a,d_j} =\\sum_{i:k_i\\ge a}b_{ij}e^{(i)}_{a,k_i}. \\) The coefficient of the top exterior monomial in \\( \\Omega=\\prod_{j=1}^nF_j^*\\alpha(d_j,l_j) \\) is, up to an overall sign …", "statement": "<p>**Computation of the intersection number.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use all the assumptions of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose columns \\(j_1,\\ldots,j_r\\) satisfying (2.2), and define \\(l_j\\) as there.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(e^{(i)}_{a,k_i}\\) for the generator pulled back from the \\(i\\)-th factor of \\(D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(1\\le a\\le k_1\\), set\n  \\[\n  t_a=|\\{i:k_i\\ge a\\}|.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(1\\le a\\le d_j\\),\n\\[\nF_j^*e_{a,d_j}\n=\\sum_{i:k_i\\ge a}b_{ij}e^{(i)}_{a,k_i}.\n\\tag{2.8}\n\\]\nThe coefficient of the top exterior monomial in\n\\[\n\\Omega=\\prod_{j=1}^nF_j^*\\alpha(d_j,l_j)\n\\]\nis, up to an overall sign,\n\\[\n\\left(\\prod_{j=1}^nc_{d_j,l_j}\\right)\n\\prod_{a=1}^{k_1}\n\\det B[1,\\ldots,t_a\\mid j_1,\\ldots,j_{t_a}],\n\\tag{2.9}\n\\]\nand is nonzero. Consequently \\(\\langle\\Omega,[D]\\rangle\\ne0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Primitivity, compatibility with block inclusions, and the inversion formula from Proposition 1 give (2.8). To justify multiplication here, write a block diagonal map as the product of maps acting on one block and as the identity on all others. Each positive occurrence contributes one generator and each negative occurrence its negative. Right multiplication by \\(P_j\\) is homotopic to the identity and has no effect on cohomology.</p><p>The exterior algebra \\(H^*(D;\\mathbb Q)\\) has altogether \\(\\sum_ik_i\\) generators, all of positive degree. A nonzero monomial of top degree must contain every one of them. By (2.8), pullback preserves the number of generators in each term, unless that term vanishes. It follows that any use of a shorter term from (2.7) contributes zero to \\(\\Omega\\). Thus only the product of the displayed leading terms in (2.7) needs to be computed.</p><p>For each \\(a\\), the rows with \\(k_i\\ge a\\) are \\(1,\\ldots,t_a\\), and the selected columns with \\(l_j\\ge a\\) are \\(j_1,\\ldots,j_{t_a}\\). Grouping the exterior factors of degree \\(2a-1\\) together gives\n\\[\n\\prod_{t=1}^{t_a}\n\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{t_a}b_{i,j_t}e^{(i)}_{a,k_i}\\right)\n=\n\\det B[1,\\ldots,t_a\\mid j_1,\\ldots,j_{t_a}]\n\\prod_{i=1}^{t_a}e^{(i)}_{a,k_i}.\n\\]\nConsequently the coefficient of the top monomial is (2.9), up to an overall sign. Every factor is nonzero, by (2.2) and Proposition 1. Hence \\(\\langle\\Omega,[D]\\rangle\\ne0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The functional \\(\\tau_d\\) is a faithful normal trace with \\(\\tau_d(I)=d\\).", "statement": "<p>**Spectral phase in the group von Neumann algebra.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\) be any group, including an uncountable group.</p><p class=\"li\">On \\(\\ell^2(G)\\), with standard basis \\(\\{\\delta_x:x\\in G\\}\\), define\n  \\[\n  L(a)\\delta_x=\\delta_{ax},\\qquad\n  R_a\\delta_x=\\delta_{xa^{-1}}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal M=\n  \\{T\\in B(\\ell^2(G)):TR_a=R_aT\\text{ for every }a\\in G\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(d\\ge1\\), regard \\(M_d(\\mathcal M)\\) as operators on \\(\\mathbb C^d\\otimes\\ell^2(G)\\), and define\n  \\[\n  \\tau_d(T)=\\sum_{j=1}^d(T_{jj})_{1,1},\n  \\qquad \\tau=\\tau_1.\n  \\tag{3.1}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define the bounded Borel function\n  \\[\n  h(e^{2\\pi it})=t-\\lfloor t\\rfloor\n  \\]\n  and, for a unitary \\(U\\in M_d(\\mathcal M)\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\ell(U)=\\tau_d(h(U)).\n  \\tag{3.3}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The functional \\(\\tau_d\\) is a faithful normal trace with \\(\\tau_d(I)=d\\). The phase is nonnegative, invariant under unitary conjugation, and additive on direct sums, and\n\\[\n\\ell(U)=0\\iff U=I.\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\nFor unitaries \\(U,V\\in M_d(\\mathcal M)\\),\n\\[\n\\ell(UV)\\le\\ell(U)+\\ell(V).\n\\]\nFor every fixed \\(c\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n\\lim_{\\epsilon\\downarrow0}\\ell(e^{2\\pi ic\\epsilon}U)=\\ell(U),\n\\tag{3.7}\n\\]\nwhereas\n\\[\n\\ell(e^{-2\\pi i\\epsilon}I_d)=d(1-\\epsilon)\n\\qquad(0&lt;\\epsilon&lt;1).\n\\]</p><p>If \\(a\\ge1\\), \\(Q_1,\\ldots,Q_a\\in\\mathcal M\\) are unitary, and\n\\[\nT(e_t\\otimes v)=e_{t+1}\\otimes Q_tv,\\qquad e_{a+1}=e_1,\n\\]\nthen, for \\(C=Q_aQ_{a-1}\\cdots Q_1\\),\n\\[\n\\ell(T)=\\frac{a-1}{2}+\\tau(h(C)).\n\\tag{3.8}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 3.1 establishes the trace, functional calculus, and basic phase properties. Lemma 3.2 proves subadditivity, and Lemma 3.3 computes the phase of a weighted cyclic shift.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The functional \\(\\tau_d\\) is a faithful normal trace.", "statement": "<p>**The trace and the phase cut.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(G\\), \\(\\mathcal M\\), \\(\\tau_d\\), \\(h\\), and \\(\\ell\\) from Proposition 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The functional \\(\\tau_d\\) is a faithful normal trace. For a unitary \\(U\\), its spectral projections define a finite measure\n\\[\n\\mu_U(E)=\\tau_d(1_E(U))\n\\]\non the unit circle \\(\\mathbb T\\), satisfying\n\\[\n\\tau_d(f(U))=\\int_{\\mathbb T}f\\,d\\mu_U,\n\\qquad \\mu_U(\\mathbb T)=d.\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nUniformly bounded pointwise convergence of Borel functions implies convergence of these trace evaluations.</p><p>The phase has all the nonnegativity, identity-detection, conjugation, direct-sum, and one-sided limit properties asserted in Proposition 3.</p>", "proof": "<p>Left and right translations commute, so every \\(L(a)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal M\\).</p><p>If \\(T\\in\\mathcal M\\) and\n\\[\nT\\delta_1=\\sum_{x\\in G}t_x\\delta_x,\n\\]\ncommutation with right translations gives\n\\[\nT_{x,y}=t_{xy^{-1}}.\n\\]\nFor \\(A,B\\in\\mathcal M\\), with corresponding coefficient families \\(a,b\\),\n\\[\n\\tau(AB)\n=\\sum_{x\\in G}a_{x^{-1}}b_x\n=\\sum_{x\\in G}b_{x^{-1}}a_x\n=\\tau(BA).\n\\]\nThese sums converge absolutely by Cauchy–Schwarz; each square-summable family has countable support. Summing over the matrix indices proves traciality of \\(\\tau_d\\).</p><p>Positivity and normality follow from its expression as a finite sum of vector functionals. If \\(T\\ge0\\) and \\(\\tau_d(T)=0\\), then\n\\[\n0=\\sum_{j=1}^d\n\\|T^{1/2}(e_j\\otimes\\delta_1)\\|^2.\n\\]\nThe square root commutes with all simultaneous right translations, so it kills \\(e_j\\otimes\\delta_x\\) for every \\(j,x\\). Thus \\(T=0\\), proving faithfulness.</p><p>The bounded Borel functional calculus of a normal operator in \\(M_d(\\mathcal M)\\) remains in this algebra. The spectral theorem gives (3.2), and bounded convergence gives the assertion about trace evaluations.</p><p>The chosen function satisfies \\(h(1)=0\\) and is right-continuous in the angle. Since\n\\[\nh(U)\\ge0,\\qquad U=\\exp(2\\pi ih(U)),\n\\]\nfaithfulness gives\n\\[\n\\ell(U)\\ge0,\\qquad \\ell(U)=0\\iff U=I.\n\\]\nThe phase is invariant under unitary conjugation and additive on direct sums. On scalar matrices, viewed as matrices over \\(\\mathcal M\\), it is the sum of the phases of the eigenvalues, counted with multiplicity.</p><p>For fixed \\(c\\ge0\\), right-continuity gives\n\\[\nh(e^{2\\pi ic\\epsilon}\\lambda)\\longrightarrow h(\\lambda)\n\\]\nfor every \\(\\lambda\\in\\mathbb T\\). Bounded convergence in \\(\\mu_U\\) proves (3.7). In the opposite direction the identity has a jump:\n\\[\n\\ell(e^{-2\\pi i\\epsilon}I_d)=d(1-\\epsilon)\n\\qquad(0&lt;\\epsilon&lt;1).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\ell(UV)\\le\\ell(U)+\\ell(V). \\)", "statement": "<p>**Spectral-phase subadditivity (original Lemma 3.1).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\) be any group and \\(d\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(\\mathcal M\\), \\(\\tau_d\\), and \\(\\ell\\) from Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U,V\\in M_d(\\mathcal M)\\) be unitary.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\ell(UV)\\le\\ell(U)+\\ell(V).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(D=h(V)\\) and join \\(U\\) to \\(UV\\) by\n\\[\nW_u=Ue^{2\\pi iuD},\\qquad 0\\le u\\le1.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(f\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb T)\\), write\n\\[\nf'(e^{2\\pi it})=\\frac{d}{dt}f(e^{2\\pi it}).\n\\]\nWe first prove\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{du}\\tau_d(f(W_u))\n=\\tau_d(f'(W_u)D).\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nIndeed, \\(W_u'=2\\pi iW_uD\\). The product and inverse rules, followed by cyclic permutation inside the trace, give, for every integer \\(k\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{du}\\tau_d(W_u^k)\n=2\\pi ik\\,\\tau_d(W_u^kD).\n\\]\nThis proves (3.5) for Laurent polynomials. For smooth \\(f\\), its Fourier coefficients satisfy\n\\[\n\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}(1+|k|)|\\widehat f(k)|&lt;\\infty,\n\\]\nwhile\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\frac{d}{du}W_u^k\\right\\|\n\\le2\\pi|k|\\|D\\|.\n\\]\nThus the Fourier series and its differentiated series converge uniformly in operator norm, proving (3.5).</p><p>If \\(f\\) is real-valued and \\(f'\\le1\\), then \\(I-f'(W_u)\\ge0\\). Although \\(D\\) need not commute with \\(W_u\\), traciality gives\n\\[\n\\tau_d(D)-\\tau_d(f'(W_u)D)\n=\n\\tau_d\\bigl(D^{1/2}(I-f'(W_u))D^{1/2}\\bigr)\\ge0.\n\\]\nIntegrating (3.5) therefore yields\n\\[\n\\tau_d(f(UV))-\\tau_d(f(U))\\le\\tau_d(D).\n\\tag{3.6}\n\\]</p><p>For \\(0&lt;\\epsilon&lt;1\\), choose a nonnegative smooth function \\(\\rho_\\epsilon\\) supported in \\((0,\\epsilon)\\) with integral one, and define\n\\[\nf_\\epsilon(e^{2\\pi it})\n=\\int_0^\\epsilon\n\\rho_\\epsilon(s)h(e^{2\\pi i(t+s)})\\,ds.\n\\]\nThis is smooth on the circle, with \\(0\\le f_\\epsilon\\le1\\). For \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), the fractional-part function satisfies\n\\[\nh(e^{2\\pi i(t+\\delta)})-h(e^{2\\pi it})\\le\\delta.\n\\]\nAveraging and taking the difference quotient gives \\(f_\\epsilon'\\le1\\).</p><p>Right-continuity gives \\(f_\\epsilon(\\lambda)\\to h(\\lambda)\\) for every \\(\\lambda\\in\\mathbb T\\). At the cut this follows explicitly from\n\\[\n0\\le f_\\epsilon(1)\\le\\epsilon;\n\\]\nsymmetric smoothing would not have this property.</p><p>Apply (3.6) to \\(f_\\epsilon\\) and pass to the limit using (3.2). Since \\(\\tau_d(D)=\\ell(V)\\), the result follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\ell(T)=\\frac{a-1}{2}+\\tau(h(C)). \\)", "statement": "<p>**Weighted-cycle formula (original Lemma 3.2).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(a\\ge1\\), and let \\(Q_1,\\ldots,Q_a\\in\\mathcal M\\) be unitary.</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(T\\in M_a(\\mathcal M)\\) by\n  \\[\n  T(e_t\\otimes v)=e_{t+1}\\otimes Q_tv\n  \\quad(1\\le t\\le a),\\qquad e_{a+1}=e_1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C=Q_aQ_{a-1}\\cdots Q_1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\ell(T)=\\frac{a-1}{2}+\\tau(h(C)).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(\\zeta=e^{2\\pi i/a}\\). Conjugating \\(T\\) by the scalar diagonal matrix with entries\n\\[\n1,\\zeta,\\ldots,\\zeta^{a-1}\n\\]\ngives \\(\\zeta T\\). Hence \\(\\ell(\\zeta^jT)=\\ell(T)\\) for every integer \\(j\\).</p><p>The scalar identity\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=0}^{a-1}h(\\zeta^j\\lambda)\n=\\frac{a-1}{2}+h(\\lambda^a),\n\\qquad \\lambda\\in\\mathbb T,\n\\]\nfollows by listing the fractional parts of the \\(a\\) equally spaced angles; it holds also when one of them is zero.</p><p>Applying bounded Borel functional calculus and taking the unnormalized trace gives\n\\[\na\\ell(T)=\\frac{a(a-1)}2+\\tau_a(h(T^a)).\n\\]\nThe operator \\(T^a\\) is diagonal. Its first diagonal entry is \\(C\\), and the other entries are the products around the same cycle with different starting coordinates. Consecutive such products are conjugate by the corresponding \\(Q_t\\), so every diagonal entry has phase trace \\(\\tau(h(C))\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\tau_a(h(T^a))=a\\tau(h(C)),\n\\]\nwhich proves the formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If all boundary words except possibly one are trivial in \\(G\\), then every boundary word is trivial in \\(G\\).", "statement": "<p>**Planar boundary obstruction (original Proposition 4.1).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\) be a group and let \\(w_1,\\ldots,w_m\\in G*F_n\\) have exponent-sum matrix \\(A=(a_{ij})\\) of row rank \\(m\\) over \\(\\mathbb Q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">After cyclic conjugation, write\n  \\[\n  w_i=x_{\\nu_{i,1}}^{s_{i,1}}g_{i,1}\\cdots\n      x_{\\nu_{i,N_i}}^{s_{i,N_i}}g_{i,N_i},\n  \\tag{4.1}\n  \\]\n  where \\(N_i\\ge1\\), \\(g_{i,t}\\in G\\), \\(s_{i,t}\\in\\{1,-1\\}\\), and \\(1\\le\\nu_{i,t}\\le n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A word disk of type \\(i\\) and sign \\(+\\) or \\(-\\) is an oriented disk whose boundary reads \\(w_i\\) or \\(w_i^{-1}\\), respectively. Variable letters occupy disjoint closed intervals, called slots, separated by nonempty corner arcs.</p><p class=\"li\">Index the slots on either sign of disk by \\(t\\) in (4.1), cyclically modulo \\(N_i\\). The boundary data are\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\n  \\text{disk sign}&amp;\\text{successor of }t&amp;\n  \\text{variable at }t&amp;\\text{corner after }t\\\\ \\hline\n  +&amp;t+1&amp;x_{\\nu_{i,t}}^{s_{i,t}}&amp;g_{i,t}\\\\\n  -&amp;t-1&amp;x_{\\nu_{i,t}}^{-s_{i,t}}&amp;g_{i,t-1}^{-1}.\n  \\end{array}\n  \\tag{4.2}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Sigma\\) be a connected oriented genus-zero surface obtained from a nonempty finite collection of these signed word disks by attaching rectangular bands. Every slot is used once; each band pairs slots with the same variable index and opposite traversal signs, and the orientations extend across the bands.</p><p class=\"li\">A boundary word of \\(\\Sigma\\) is read from its corner labels; band sides contribute no label. Changing the starting corner conjugates the word.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If all boundary words except possibly one are trivial in \\(G\\), then every boundary word is trivial in \\(G\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose the possibly exceptional boundary component \\(z_0\\). Lemma 4.1 gives balanced disk counts and, for every \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\), a phase distribution realizing\n\\[\n\\eta_z=-\\epsilon\\quad(z\\ne z_0),\\qquad\n\\eta_{z_0}=(q-1)\\epsilon.\n\\]</p><p>For each such distribution, Lemma 4.2 gives\n\\[\n\\ell(S\\Theta)\\le\\frac32b-K.\n\\]\nTaking the limit supplied by Lemma 4.3 forces\n\\[\n\\tau\\bigl(h(L(r_{z_0}^{-1}))\\bigr)=0.\n\\]\nFaithfulness of \\(\\tau\\) and nonnegativity of \\(h\\), as established in Proposition 3, imply\n\\[\nL(r_{z_0}^{-1})=I.\n\\]\nApplying this operator to \\(\\delta_1\\) gives \\(r_{z_0}=1\\). All other boundary words were assumed trivial.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The counts satisfy \\(p_i=m_i\\).", "statement": "<p>**Balanced disk counts, boundary cycles, and distribution of phases.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the surface and words of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p_i\\) and \\(m_i\\) be the numbers of positive and negative disks of type \\(i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathscr D\\) be the set of slots.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\sigma\\) be the successor permutation along the oriented disk boundaries, and let \\(\\theta\\) be the fixed-point-free involution pairing band ends.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(d\\in\\mathscr D\\), let \\(c_d\\in G\\) label the corner from the end of \\(d\\) to the start of \\(\\sigma d\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The counts satisfy \\(p_i=m_i\\). Let \\(I\\) be the set of types present and put\n\\[\nk_i=p_i=m_i\\ge1,\\qquad\nK=\\sum_{i\\in I}k_i,\\qquad\nb=\\sum_{i\\in I}N_ik_i.\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nThen \\(\\Sigma\\) has \\(2K\\) disks, \\(2b\\) slots, and \\(b\\) bands.</p><p>The cycles of \\(\\sigma\\theta\\) are exactly the boundary components. For such a cycle \\(z=(d_1,\\ldots,d_a)\\), put\n\\[\na(z)=a,\\qquad r_z=c_{\\theta d_1}\\cdots c_{\\theta d_a}.\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\nIf \\(q\\) is the number of these cycles, then \\(q\\ge1\\) and\n\\[\n2-q=\\chi(\\Sigma)=2K-b,\\qquad\n\\sum_za(z)=2b.\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]</p><p>For every family \\((\\eta_z)_z\\) of real numbers satisfying \\(\\sum_z\\eta_z=0\\), there are real numbers \\((\\gamma_d)_{d\\in\\mathscr D}\\) such that\n\\[\n\\gamma_{\\theta d}=-\\gamma_d,\\qquad\n\\sum_{d\\in z}\\gamma_d=\\eta_z.\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Each band pairs opposite occurrences of the same variable, whence\n\\[\n\\sum_i(p_i-m_i)a_{ij}=0\n\\qquad(1\\le j\\le n).\n\\]\nRow independence gives \\(p_i=m_i\\), and the disk, slot, and band counts follow.</p><p>Starting at the start of slot \\(d\\), a boundary path crosses a band side to the end of \\(\\theta d\\), then follows its corner to the start of \\(\\sigma\\theta d\\). Consequently the cycles of \\(\\sigma\\theta\\) are exactly the boundary components, and their words are (4.4).</p><p>The nonempty corner arcs ensure that there is at least one boundary component. Genus zero gives\n\\[\n2-q=\\chi(\\Sigma)=2K-b,\n\\]\nand every slot belongs to exactly one cycle, giving \\(\\sum_za(z)=2b\\).</p><p>To prove (4.6), form a graph whose vertices are the boundary cycles and whose edges are the pairs \\(\\{d,\\theta d\\}\\), with an end incident to the cycle containing that slot. Loops and multiple edges are allowed.</p><p>Connectedness of \\(\\Sigma\\) means that \\(\\sigma\\) and \\(\\theta\\) act transitively on \\(\\mathscr D\\). Since \\(\\sigma\\theta\\) and \\(\\theta\\) generate the same permutation group, this graph is connected.</p><p>Choose a spanning tree and set the values on all other edges to zero. At a leaf, set the value at its end of the remaining edge equal to its prescribed sum and the value at the other end to its negative; remove the leaf and adjust the prescribed sum at its neighbor. The zero total ensures that the last vertex is satisfied. This proves (4.6), including the one-vertex case.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\ell(S\\Theta)\\le\\frac32b-K. \\) This holds for every such phase distribution.", "statement": "<p>**The upper phase bound.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the surface of Proposition 4 and the balanced counts, slot permutations, and corner labels of Lemma 4.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Thus \\(k_i\\) is the number of disks of either sign of type \\(i\\),\n  \\[\n  K=\\sum_{i\\in I}k_i,\\qquad b=\\sum_{i\\in I}N_ik_i,\n  \\qquad |\\mathscr D|=2b.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let real numbers \\(\\gamma_d\\) satisfy \\(\\gamma_{\\theta d}=-\\gamma_d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In \\(M_{2b}(\\mathcal M)\\), acting on \\(\\mathbb C^{\\mathscr D}\\otimes\\ell^2(G)\\), define\n  \\[\n  S(e_d\\otimes v)=e_{\\sigma d}\\otimes L(c_d^{-1})v,\n  \\qquad\n  \\Theta(e_d\\otimes v)=e^{2\\pi i\\gamma_d}e_{\\theta d}\\otimes v.\n  \\tag{4.7}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the phase of Proposition 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\ell(S\\Theta)\\le\\frac32b-K.\n\\tag{4.11}\n\\]\nThis holds for every such phase distribution.</p>", "proof": "<p>Both \\(S\\) and \\(\\Theta\\) are unitary, and \\(\\Theta^2=I\\). Scalar matrices are identified with their tensor products with the identity on \\(\\ell^2(G)\\); their trace is therefore the ordinary matrix trace.</p><p>For each \\(i\\in I\\), order the \\(k_i\\) disks of each sign. At each position \\(t\\), their slots form two coordinate spaces, each identified with \\(\\mathbb C^{k_i}\\). Given \\(X_i\\in U(k_i)\\), define a scalar unitary involution \\(J\\) by sending the positive array at \\((i,t)\\) to the negative array by \\(X_i\\), and the negative array back by \\(X_i^{-1}\\). Use the same \\(X_i\\) at every position of type \\(i\\).</p><p>The corner conventions give\n\\[\nJSJ=S^{-1}.\n\\tag{4.8}\n\\]\nIndeed, on positive arrays \\(S\\) moves from \\(t\\) to \\(t+1\\) with coefficient \\(L(g_{i,t}^{-1})\\), whereas on negative arrays it moves from \\(t\\) to \\(t-1\\) with coefficient \\(L(g_{i,t-1})\\). Applying \\(J\\) on either side interchanges these rules; its factors \\(X_i\\) and \\(X_i^{-1}\\) cancel because the coefficients are constant across each array and commute with scalar matrices.</p><p>Thus \\(SJ\\) is a unitary involution. It exchanges the two disk signs, so its diagonal blocks, and hence its trace, are zero. Its \\(-1\\) spectral projection \\((I-SJ)/2\\) has trace \\(b\\), giving\n\\[\n\\ell(SJ)=\\frac b2.\n\\tag{4.9}\n\\]</p><p>We now choose the \\(X_i\\) so that \\(J\\Theta\\) has many fixed vectors. Regroup the scalar coordinates by variable index \\(j\\) and traversal sign into \\(V_{j,+}\\) and \\(V_{j,-}\\). For each occurrence \\((i,t)\\) with \\(\\nu_{i,t}=j\\), each space has one block of dimension \\(k_i\\): the positive or negative disk array is selected according to its traversal sign. Both spaces have dimension\n\\[\nd_j=\\sum_{\\substack{i\\in I,\\ 1\\le t\\le N_i\\\\\\nu_{i,t}=j}}k_i,\n\\qquad \\sum_jd_j=b.\n\\]</p><p>Both \\(J\\) and \\(\\Theta\\) exchange \\(V_{j,+}\\) and \\(V_{j,-}\\). Let \\(P_j\\in U(d_j)\\) represent\n\\[\n\\Theta:V_{j,+}\\longrightarrow V_{j,-}\n\\]\nunder the array coordinates. On the return map \\(J:V_{j,-}\\to V_{j,+}\\), the block at \\((i,t)\\) is \\(X_i^{-s_{i,t}}\\). Hence\n\\[\n(J\\Theta)|_{V_{j,+}}\n=\n\\operatorname{diag}_{(i,t):\\nu_{i,t}=j}(X_i^{-s_{i,t}})P_j.\n\\tag{4.10}\n\\]</p><p>The signed block counts are\n\\[\n\\sum_{t:\\nu_{i,t}=j}(-s_{i,t})=-a_{ij}.\n\\]\nThe matrix formed by the rows of \\(-A\\) indexed by \\(I\\) has full row rank. Apply Proposition 2, after ordering the \\(k_i\\), and then the intersection interpretation in Proposition 1. They supply \\(X_i\\) for which\n\\[\n\\dim_{\\mathbb C}\n\\ker\\left((J\\Theta-I)|_{\\bigoplus_jV_{j,+}}\\right)\\ge K.\n\\]</p><p>There are equally many fixed vectors on \\(\\bigoplus_jV_{j,-}\\): \\(J\\) interchanges the two sums and satisfies\n\\[\nJ(J\\Theta)J=(J\\Theta)^{-1}.\n\\]\nThus the full fixed-space dimension \\(f\\) of the scalar matrix \\(J\\Theta\\) is at least \\(2K\\).</p><p>The same conjugacy makes its eigenvalue multiset invariant under \\(\\lambda\\mapsto\\lambda^{-1}\\). Since\n\\[\nh(\\lambda)+h(\\lambda^{-1})=\n\\begin{cases}\n1,&amp;\\lambda\\ne1,\\\\\n0,&amp;\\lambda=1,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nwe have\n\\[\n2\\ell(J\\Theta)=2b-f\\le2b-2K.\n\\]\nUsing\n\\[\nS\\Theta=(SJ)(J\\Theta),\n\\]\n(4.9), and subadditivity from Proposition 3 gives\n\\[\n\\ell(S\\Theta)\\le\\frac b2+(b-K)=\\frac32b-K.\n\\]\nThe auxiliary choice of \\(J\\) may depend on the phase distribution.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The restriction \\(T_z\\) of \\(S\\Theta\\) to the coordinates of \\(z\\) satisfies \\( \\ell(T_z) =\\frac{a(z)-1}{2} +\\tau\\bigl(h(e^{2\\pi i\\eta_z}L(r_z^{-1}))\\bigr). \\) If \\(z_0\\) is a boundary component and \\(r_z=1\\) for every …", "statement": "<p>**The boundary formula and its one-sided limit.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the surface, boundary cycles \\(z\\), words \\(r_z\\), lengths \\(a(z)\\), and numbers \\(K,b,q\\) of Lemma 4.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the operators \\(S,\\Theta\\) of Lemma 4.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\gamma_d\\) satisfy (4.6) for real numbers \\(\\eta_z\\) with \\(\\sum_z\\eta_z=0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The restriction \\(T_z\\) of \\(S\\Theta\\) to the coordinates of \\(z\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\ell(T_z)\n=\\frac{a(z)-1}{2}\n+\\tau\\bigl(h(e^{2\\pi i\\eta_z}L(r_z^{-1}))\\bigr).\n\\tag{4.12}\n\\]</p><p>If \\(z_0\\) is a boundary component and \\(r_z=1\\) for every \\(z\\ne z_0\\), choose\n\\[\n\\eta_z=-\\epsilon\\quad(z\\ne z_0),\\qquad\n\\eta_{z_0}=(q-1)\\epsilon.\n\\]\nFor any corresponding choices of \\(\\gamma_d\\),\n\\[\n\\lim_{\\epsilon\\downarrow0}\\ell(S\\Theta)\n=\n\\frac32b-K+\\tau\\bigl(h(L(r_{z_0}^{-1}))\\bigr).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>On the coordinates of a cycle \\(z=(d_1,\\ldots,d_a)\\) of \\(\\sigma\\theta\\), the operator \\(S\\Theta\\) is a weighted cyclic shift. Its step from \\(d_t\\) to \\(d_{t+1}\\) has weight\n\\[\ne^{2\\pi i\\gamma_{d_t}}L(c_{\\theta d_t}^{-1}).\n\\]\nThe circuit starting at \\(d_1\\) therefore has weight\n\\[\ne^{2\\pi i\\sum_{d\\in z}\\gamma_d}\nL(c_{\\theta d_a}^{-1}\\cdots c_{\\theta d_1}^{-1})\n=\ne^{2\\pi i\\eta_z}L(r_z^{-1}).\n\\]\nThe weighted-cycle formula of Proposition 3 gives (4.12).</p><p>For \\(z\\ne z_0\\), the assumption \\(r_z=1\\) makes the last term in (4.12) equal to \\(1-\\epsilon\\) when \\(0&lt;\\epsilon&lt;1\\). For \\(z_0\\), the scalar phase approaches zero from the nonnegative side. Thus\n\\[\nh(e^{2\\pi i(q-1)\\epsilon}\\lambda)\\longrightarrow h(\\lambda)\n\\]\nat every point of the unit circle, including \\(\\lambda=1\\). Bounded convergence in the spectral measure gives\n\\[\n\\tau\\bigl(h(e^{2\\pi i(q-1)\\epsilon}L(r_{z_0}^{-1}))\\bigr)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\tau\\bigl(h(L(r_{z_0}^{-1}))\\bigr).\n\\]\nThis also covers \\(q=1\\), when the scalar phase is identically zero.</p><p>Summing (4.12) over all cycles and using (4.5), we obtain\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{\\epsilon\\downarrow0}\\ell(S\\Theta)\n&amp;=\\frac{2b-q}{2}+q-1\n  +\\tau\\bigl(h(L(r_{z_0}^{-1}))\\bigr)\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac32b-K+\\tau\\bigl(h(L(r_{z_0}^{-1}))\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are an oriented polygonal disk \\(\\Delta\\), disjoint polygonal word disks \\(D_\\alpha\\subset\\operatorname{int}\\Delta\\) of type \\(i_\\alpha\\) and sign \\(\\epsilon_\\alpha\\), and mutually disjoint rectangular bands …", "statement": "<p>**Planar surfaces arising from a normal-closure expression.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\) be a group and \\(F_n=F(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(w_1,\\ldots,w_m\\in G*F_n\\) be written in the cyclic form (4.1), with \\(N_i\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(g\\in G\\), and suppose that in \\(G*F_n\\)\n  \\[\n  g=\\prod_{\\alpha=1}^u\n  b_\\alpha w_{i_\\alpha}^{\\epsilon_\\alpha}b_\\alpha^{-1},\n  \\qquad\n  b_\\alpha\\in G*F_n,\\quad\n  \\epsilon_\\alpha\\in\\{1,-1\\},\\quad u&gt;0.\n  \\tag{5.1}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a based CW presentation space \\(Y\\) with \\(\\pi_1(Y)=G\\), and choose based loops for the coefficients and for \\(g\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are an oriented polygonal disk \\(\\Delta\\), disjoint polygonal word disks \\(D_\\alpha\\subset\\operatorname{int}\\Delta\\) of type \\(i_\\alpha\\) and sign \\(\\epsilon_\\alpha\\), and mutually disjoint rectangular bands between their slots such that:</p><p class=\"li\">every variable occurrence has exactly one slot, and every slot is paired;</p><p class=\"li\">each band pairs the same variable with opposite traversal signs;</p><p class=\"li\">their union is a compact surface \\(\\Sigma\\subset\\operatorname{int}\\Delta\\), every connected component of which is planar;</p><p class=\"li\">on\n  \\[\n  P=\\Delta\\setminus\\bigcup_{\\alpha=1}^u\\operatorname{int}D_\\alpha\n  \\]\n  there is a coefficient map \\(v:P\\to Y\\), equal to the chosen loop for \\(g\\) on \\(\\partial\\Delta\\), constant at the basepoint on every band, and reading the prescribed coefficient labels between slots;</p><p class=\"li\">the boundary words of every component of \\(\\Sigma\\) are exactly the loops supplied by \\(v\\) on its boundary circles.</p><p>No asphericity or finiteness property of \\(Y\\) is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 5.1 constructs the punctured disk map realizing the normal-closure expression. Lemma 5.2 produces disjoint circle levels with coefficient projection constant nearby. Lemma 5.3 thickens their proper arcs into the required bands and verifies the surface and boundary-label properties.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "One can choose an oriented polygonal disk \\(\\Delta\\), disjoint interior polygonal disks \\(D_\\alpha\\), and a map \\( f:P=\\Delta\\setminus\\bigcup_{\\alpha=1}^u\\operatorname{int}D_\\alpha \\longrightarrow Y' \\) such that:", "statement": "<p>**A punctured disk carrying the relation.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(G\\), \\(w_i\\), \\(g\\), \\(b_\\alpha\\), \\(\\epsilon_\\alpha\\), \\(u\\), and \\(Y\\) from Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  Y'=Y\\vee S_1^1\\vee\\cdots\\vee S_n^1,\n  \\]\n  where the added circles represent the variables, so \\(\\pi_1(Y')=G*F_n\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One can choose an oriented polygonal disk \\(\\Delta\\), disjoint interior polygonal disks \\(D_\\alpha\\), and a map\n\\[\nf:P=\\Delta\\setminus\\bigcup_{\\alpha=1}^u\\operatorname{int}D_\\alpha\n\\longrightarrow Y'\n\\]\nsuch that:</p><p class=\"li\">the outer boundary reads the chosen loop for \\(g\\) in \\(Y\\);</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\partial D_\\alpha\\), oriented as the boundary of the missing disk, reads \\(w_{i_\\alpha}^{\\epsilon_\\alpha}\\);</p><p class=\"li\">every variable letter is represented by one monotone traversal of its circle;</p><p class=\"li\">intervening coefficient intervals map to the chosen loops in \\(Y\\), retaining an interval even for an identity coefficient.</p>", "proof": "<p>Cut \\(P\\) along disjoint arcs from the outer boundary to the holes, and map those arcs to the loops for \\(b_\\alpha\\). Place their outer endpoints in a constant portion of the outer loop, with the holes encountered in reverse order after the outer traversal. The boundary of the cut disk reads\n\\[\ng\\bigl(b_uw_{i_u}^{\\epsilon_u}b_u^{-1}\\bigr)^{-1}\n\\cdots\n\\bigl(b_1w_{i_1}^{\\epsilon_1}b_1^{-1}\\bigr)^{-1}.\n\\]\nIt is nullhomotopic by (5.1). A nullhomotopy extends the map across the cut disk. The two copies of each cut arc have the same map with opposite traversal, so the extension descends to \\(P\\).</p><p>The induced boundary orientation of \\(P\\) on a hole is opposite to the missing-disk orientation; this explains the inverses in the displayed word. The prescribed variable and coefficient boundary maps are therefore realized.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are a common finite triangulation of \\(P\\), piecewise affine circle maps \\(\\widetilde f_j\\), and values \\(t_j\\in(1/3,2/3)\\) such that …", "statement": "<p>**Disjoint circle levels with constant coefficient projection nearby.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f:P\\to Y'\\) have the boundary values in Lemma 5.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f_j:P\\to S_j^1=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z\\) be the projection obtained by collapsing every other wedge summand.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(v:P\\to Y\\) be the coefficient projection of \\(f\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are a common finite triangulation of \\(P\\), piecewise affine circle maps \\(\\widetilde f_j\\), and values \\(t_j\\in(1/3,2/3)\\) such that\n\\[\n\\widetilde f_j|_{\\partial P}=f_j|_{\\partial P},\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{dist}(\\widetilde f_j(p),f_j(p))&lt;\\frac1{12}\n\\quad(p\\in P),\n\\]\nand:</p><p class=\"li\">\\(t_j\\) is not the image of any vertex under \\(\\widetilde f_j\\);</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\widetilde f_j^{-1}(t_j)\\) is a finite disjoint union of polygonal circles and properly embedded polygonal arcs;</p><p class=\"li\">these level sets are disjoint for different \\(j\\);</p><p class=\"li\">there is exactly one level-set endpoint for each occurrence of \\(x_j^{\\pm1}\\) on a hole, and none on the outer boundary or on a coefficient interval;</p><p class=\"li\">\\(v\\) is constant at the basepoint on a neighborhood of each level set.</p>", "proof": "<p>The boundary maps are already piecewise affine in the angular coordinate. Subdivide to respect their breakpoints and so finely that the image of each triangle under each \\(f_j\\) lies in a short arc of the circle. Lift that arc to \\(\\mathbb R\\) and interpolate its vertex values. The lifts on a shared edge differ by an integer, so the interpolants agree as circle maps. Uniform continuity gives the error bound \\(1/12\\).</p><p>Choose \\(t_j\\in(1/3,2/3)\\) that is not the image of any vertex under \\(\\widetilde f_j\\). On each triangle the level set consists of line segments, and avoidance of vertices makes the segments join without branching. It is therefore a finite disjoint union of polygonal circles and properly embedded polygonal arcs.</p><p>Because the approximation is fixed on the boundary, there is exactly one endpoint for each occurrence of \\(x_j^{\\pm1}\\) on a hole, and no endpoint on the outer boundary or on a coefficient interval.</p><p>At a point of \\(\\widetilde f_j^{-1}(t_j)\\), the original \\(f_j\\) is at distance more than \\(1/4\\) from the circle basepoint. Thus \\(f\\) lands in the \\(j\\)-th variable circle away from the wedge point, and all its other circle projections vanish. The same remains true in a neighborhood of the level set. In particular, \\(v\\) is constant at the basepoint on that neighborhood.</p><p>This also shows that the level sets are disjoint for different indices. The argument uses the original wedge-valued map \\(f\\); the separately approximated maps need not themselves combine to a map into the wedge.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After discarding the closed level curves, the proper level arcs admit mutually disjoint thin rectangular neighborhoods that form bands satisfying all the band, slot, planarity, and coefficient-label conclusions of …", "statement": "<p>**Bands obtained from proper level arcs.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the punctured disk map \\(f\\), coefficient projection \\(v\\), and disjoint polygonal level sets of Lemmas 5.1 and 5.2.</p><p class=\"li\">The boundary of each missing disk has the orientation used to read \\(w_{i_\\alpha}^{\\epsilon_\\alpha}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After discarding the closed level curves, the proper level arcs admit mutually disjoint thin rectangular neighborhoods that form bands satisfying all the band, slot, planarity, and coefficient-label conclusions of Proposition 5.</p>", "proof": "<p>Discard the closed level curves and choose thin, mutually disjoint rectangular neighborhoods of the proper arcs inside the neighborhoods where \\(v\\) is constant. These are the bands. Each meets the holes in small intervals inside the corresponding variable traversals, and every occurrence has exactly one such interval.</p><p>Coorientation of a circle level makes the two endpoint crossing signs of an arc opposite with respect to the boundary orientation of \\(P\\). Reversing both orientations to the missing-disk orientations leaves the signs opposite. Hence each band pairs the same variable with opposite traversal signs.</p><p>Use the smaller attachment intervals as the slots. The pieces of a variable traversal outside its slot project constantly to \\(Y\\), so shrinking the slots does not change any coefficient label between them. Moreover, \\(v\\) is constant on every band.</p><p>Reinsert the disks \\(D_\\alpha\\) as the signed word disks. Their union with the bands is a compact surface embedded in the interior of \\(\\Delta\\), with every slot paired. Every connected component is planar, and its boundary words are exactly the loops supplied by \\(v\\) on those boundary circles.</p>"}], "edges": [[2, 1, "cite"], [2, 6, "cite"], [2, 8, "cite"], [2, 15, "cite"], [3, 2, "cite"], [3, 5, "cite"], [4, 2, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [5, 2, "cite"], [6, 1, "cite"], [6, 15, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [8, 6, "cite"], [9, 13, "cite"], [9, 15, "cite"], [9, 16, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [10, 11, "cite"], [11, 9, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [12, 9, "cite"], [13, 0, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [14, 16, "cite"], [15, 13, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [16, 13, "cite"], [17, 0, "cite"], [18, 17, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [18, 20, "cite"], [19, 17, "cite"], [19, 20, "cite"], [20, 17, "cite"]]});
